1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
Imagine a world
where every person on Earth owns a car.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,840
Not every family. Every person.

3
00:00:07,524 --> 00:00:10,564
Eight billion people. Eight billion cars.

4
00:00:10,644 --> 00:00:14,184
No waiting for a bus.
No asking anyone for a lift.

5
00:00:14,524 --> 00:00:16,324
Your car is always there.

6
00:00:16,819 --> 00:00:18,959
It sounds like the ultimate freedom.

7
00:00:19,846 --> 00:00:22,806
A car is an excellent solution
for one person.

8
00:00:23,526 --> 00:00:26,666
But what happens
when everyone uses the same solution?

9
00:00:28,011 --> 00:00:30,291
This isn't as absurd as it sounds.

10
00:00:31,071 --> 00:00:35,151
In the United States, there are roughly
eight vehicles for every ten people.

11
00:00:35,703 --> 00:00:40,003
That's more cars, SUVs and pickups than
there are people with a driving licence.

12
00:00:41,239 --> 00:00:43,839
The rest of the world
is a long way behind.

13
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:47,779
There are more than one and a half billion
vehicles on Earth.

14
00:00:48,219 --> 00:00:50,659
Roughly one for every five people.

15
00:00:50,895 --> 00:00:53,215
So imagine a world
where every country reached

16
00:00:53,445 --> 00:00:56,015
the American level of motorization.

17
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:59,255
That's about six and a half
billion vehicles.

18
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:04,320
Close enough to one each.
So let's round it up. Eight billion cars.

19
00:01:06,505 --> 00:01:08,725
And let's say they all arrive tomorrow.

20
00:01:09,325 --> 00:01:10,905
The first problem isn't driving.

21
00:01:10,985 --> 00:01:12,185
It's parking.

22
00:01:12,683 --> 00:01:17,123
A parking space is about two and a half
metres wide and five metres long.

23
00:01:17,523 --> 00:01:19,463
Twelve and a half square metres.

24
00:01:20,568 --> 00:01:25,288
Multiply that by eight billion, and you
get a hundred thousand square kilometres.

25
00:01:25,548 --> 00:01:27,828
About the size of South Korea.

26
00:01:28,302 --> 00:01:30,482
But that's cars packed bumper to bumper.

27
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:33,322
Nobody could get in or out.

28
00:01:33,902 --> 00:01:37,802
A real car park needs lanes to drive in
and room to open the doors.

29
00:01:38,235 --> 00:01:41,755
That takes twenty-five
to thirty-three square metres per space.

30
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:45,695
Now we need somewhere between
two and three South Koreas.

31
00:01:46,075 --> 00:01:48,395
Roughly the whole United Kingdom.

32
00:01:48,866 --> 00:01:51,046
And that's just one space per car.

33
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:53,926
But a car doesn't stay in one place.

34
00:01:54,486 --> 00:01:58,586
It needs a space at home, another at work,
and another outside the shop.

35
00:01:58,842 --> 00:02:01,542
And in all of those places,
it mostly waits.

36
00:02:02,342 --> 00:02:05,862
The average car is parked
about ninety-five percent of the time.

37
00:02:06,471 --> 00:02:08,791
Those spaces can't
be in the middle of nowhere.

38
00:02:09,351 --> 00:02:11,351
They have to be right next to people.

39
00:02:11,811 --> 00:02:14,331
Homes, schools, hospitals, shops.

40
00:02:15,011 --> 00:02:17,311
Exactly where land is already scarce.

41
00:02:18,093 --> 00:02:20,713
To make room,
cities would have to give something up.

42
00:02:21,013 --> 00:02:23,933
Parks. Playgrounds. Housing.

43
00:02:24,679 --> 00:02:28,159
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

44
00:02:28,779 --> 00:02:30,399
It really helps the channel.

45
00:02:32,569 --> 00:02:35,169
Now let's take the cars
out of the car park.

46
00:02:35,929 --> 00:02:39,589
It's Monday morning,
and everyone who has a car can drive it.

47
00:02:40,155 --> 00:02:42,295
One lane of a city street,
filled with cars,

48
00:02:42,525 --> 00:02:46,955
moves somewhere between six hundred
and sixteen hundred people an hour.

49
00:02:47,504 --> 00:02:51,714
Give the same lane to buses,
and it can move up to eight thousand.

50
00:02:52,104 --> 00:02:55,201
Put trams on it,
and up to twenty-five thousand.

51
00:02:55,281 --> 00:02:57,421
That's the geometry of the problem.

52
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:00,801
A car is a wonderful way
to move one person.

53
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:03,701
It's a very bulky way to move a crowd.

54
00:03:05,047 --> 00:03:06,647
So we build more roads.

55
00:03:07,047 --> 00:03:08,587
That's what Houston did.

56
00:03:09,241 --> 00:03:14,061
Its Katy Freeway was widened to more than
twenty lanes, counting the side roads.

57
00:03:14,861 --> 00:03:19,241
It was finished in 2008,
and it cost almost three billion dollars.

58
00:03:20,198 --> 00:03:22,698
And within a few years,
the traffic came back.

59
00:03:23,438 --> 00:03:28,818
Between 2011 and 2014, the morning
commute on it got thirty percent slower.

60
00:03:29,048 --> 00:03:32,867
The evening one, fifty-five percent.

61
00:03:32,947 --> 00:03:35,387
A wider road makes driving easier.

62
00:03:35,987 --> 00:03:39,107
So more people drive,
until the new lanes fill up too.

63
00:03:39,727 --> 00:03:41,447
It's called induced demand.

64
00:03:41,999 --> 00:03:43,739
And every road ends somewhere.

65
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:46,679
At a bridge. A junction. A city centre.

66
00:03:46,759 --> 00:03:48,419
You can't widen all of them.

67
00:03:50,343 --> 00:03:52,903
And congestion isn't the only cost.

68
00:03:53,818 --> 00:03:55,918
The World Health Organization estimates

69
00:03:56,148 --> 00:04:00,878
that around one point two million people
die in road crashes every year.

70
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:04,201
We can't simply multiply that
by the number of cars.

71
00:04:05,061 --> 00:04:10,141
It depends on how far people drive,
how fast, on what roads, and in what cars.

72
00:04:10,544 --> 00:04:13,624
But a world with five times as many cars,
and far more driving,

73
00:04:13,854 --> 00:04:17,604
would make road safety
an even more important question.

74
00:04:20,164 --> 00:04:21,684
And we've skipped a step.

75
00:04:22,264 --> 00:04:26,784
Before anyone can park these cars,
or drive them, someone has to build them.

76
00:04:27,323 --> 00:04:31,443
In 2024, the world built
about ninety-three million vehicles.

77
00:04:32,223 --> 00:04:35,443
Cars, vans, trucks and buses,
all together.

78
00:04:36,104 --> 00:04:39,264
In America, a typical car lasts
around seventeen years.

79
00:04:39,494 --> 00:04:45,384
Ninety-three million a year, for seventeen
years, is about one and a half billion.

80
00:04:45,637 --> 00:04:48,677
Which is roughly the number of vehicles
the world has today.

81
00:04:48,757 --> 00:04:51,197
And the fleet is still growing.

82
00:04:51,857 --> 00:04:53,617
Keeping eight billion cars on the road

83
00:04:53,847 --> 00:04:57,117
would take about five times
as many cars every year.

84
00:04:57,697 --> 00:05:00,777
And getting there in the first place
would take even more.

85
00:05:03,027 --> 00:05:06,247
Then we need to fuel them.
Let's be modest.

86
00:05:07,187 --> 00:05:10,347
Say each car drives
ten thousand kilometres a year,

87
00:05:10,427 --> 00:05:13,247
less than a typical American
or European car.

88
00:05:13,458 --> 00:05:17,158
And say it uses seven litres of petrol
for every hundred kilometres.

89
00:05:18,298 --> 00:05:21,738
That's around ninety-six million barrels
of petrol a day.

90
00:05:22,324 --> 00:05:24,884
The whole world today uses
about a hundred and three.

91
00:05:24,964 --> 00:05:29,444
For everything.
Planes, ships, trucks, heating, plastics.

92
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:33,324
So let's make every car electric.

93
00:05:33,484 --> 00:05:35,644
No petrol. No exhaust.

94
00:05:35,874 --> 00:05:38,324
Cleaner air in every city.

95
00:05:38,966 --> 00:05:40,326
The same ten thousand kilometres

96
00:05:40,406 --> 00:05:44,966
takes about two thousand kilowatt-hours
of electricity a year.

97
00:05:46,186 --> 00:05:48,866
Times eight billion,
that's about half of all the electricity

98
00:05:49,096 --> 00:05:51,106
the world generates today.

99
00:05:51,536 --> 00:05:54,156
And every one of those cars
needs a battery.

100
00:05:55,076 --> 00:05:58,836
Just replacing them as the cars wear out
would take around thirty times

101
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:01,736
as many batteries
as the world makes today.

102
00:06:02,386 --> 00:06:07,306
Some of this would get easier.
Batteries improve. Clean power grows.

103
00:06:07,426 --> 00:06:09,186
Cars can charge at night.

104
00:06:09,804 --> 00:06:11,864
But none of it makes space unlimited.

105
00:06:12,784 --> 00:06:14,824
An electric car is still a car.

106
00:06:15,244 --> 00:06:17,704
It still needs a road,
and somewhere to park.

107
00:06:18,416 --> 00:06:21,916
We could change the engine.
We could change the fuel.

108
00:06:22,376 --> 00:06:24,956
We could even change
the person behind the wheel.

109
00:06:25,656 --> 00:06:28,456
But the geometry of the problem
would stay the same.

110
00:06:31,256 --> 00:06:33,816
And there's one question
we haven't asked yet.

111
00:06:34,016 --> 00:06:37,016
Who's actually going to drive
all these cars?

112
00:06:37,733 --> 00:06:40,373
Almost three in ten people on Earth
are children.

113
00:06:40,453 --> 00:06:43,413
Around two point four billion of them.

114
00:06:43,893 --> 00:06:46,673
In our world,
every one of them owns a car.

115
00:06:47,105 --> 00:06:49,845
Then there are people
who are too old to drive safely,

116
00:06:50,125 --> 00:06:52,385
who can't see, or who never learned.

117
00:06:53,003 --> 00:06:57,443
Even in the United States, almost three in
ten people don't have a driving licence.

118
00:06:58,817 --> 00:07:00,597
Their cars would sit there.

119
00:07:00,757 --> 00:07:02,857
Or someone else would drive them.

120
00:07:03,237 --> 00:07:05,637
But if someone else is driving anyway,

121
00:07:05,717 --> 00:07:08,617
why does every passenger
need a car of their own?

122
00:07:09,135 --> 00:07:11,415
That's where the experiment falls apart.

123
00:07:11,955 --> 00:07:13,855
We wanted to give everyone freedom.

124
00:07:14,655 --> 00:07:18,395
But we confused freedom of movement
with owning a particular machine.

125
00:07:19,015 --> 00:07:21,635
Freedom isn't the number of machines
we own.

126
00:07:22,015 --> 00:07:24,675
It's the number of places
we can actually reach.

127
00:07:27,885 --> 00:07:30,185
The answer isn't to eliminate cars.

128
00:07:30,925 --> 00:07:34,965
Cars are essential for some journeys,
especially in rural areas.

129
00:07:35,685 --> 00:07:39,565
The goal is to give people reliable ways
to get where they need to go,

130
00:07:39,645 --> 00:07:43,245
without requiring everyone
to own and drive the same machine.

131
00:07:45,785 --> 00:07:49,225
So let's go back to our world.
Eight billion people.

132
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:51,025
Eight billion cars.

133
00:07:52,151 --> 00:07:56,171
Car parks the size of a country,
standing still most of the day.

134
00:07:56,631 --> 00:07:59,691
Roads that fill up
as fast as we can widen them.

135
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:02,811
Almost as much fuel
as the whole world uses today,

136
00:08:03,041 --> 00:08:06,651
or half of today's electricity,
just to keep them moving.

137
00:08:08,701 --> 00:08:10,621
Not because cars are bad.

138
00:08:11,281 --> 00:08:13,781
The trouble starts
when everyone uses the same solution,

139
00:08:14,011 --> 00:08:16,721
in the same places, at the same time.

140
00:08:17,911 --> 00:08:20,491
A car is a great answer for one person.

141
00:08:21,071 --> 00:08:23,291
It's a strange answer for eight billion.

142
00:08:26,621 --> 00:08:28,481
So here's a question for you.

143
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:31,301
If you could get anywhere you wanted,
whenever you wanted,

144
00:08:31,531 --> 00:08:34,261
without owning a car,
would you still buy one?

145
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:36,111
Let me know in the comments.
